CONTROLS
Control     Scanners                                                 Purpose
cloud       prowler                                                  Check a live cloud account against the provider's CIS Benchmark.
dast        nuclei                                                   Find problems only visible from outside a running app (dynamic testing).
headers     draugr-headers                                           Check how a running site answers a browser, the settings that stop a page being abused.
iac         trivy-config                                             Find settings that leave a door open, in Terraform, Kubernetes and Dockerfiles.
images      trivy, grype*                                            Find known flaws in the operating system and packages baked into a container image.
kubernetes  draugr-k8s-policies, kube-bench*, kube-bench-job*        Check a Kubernetes cluster against the CIS Benchmark, rather than taking it on trust.
licenses    trivy-license, mend-licenses*                            Report license terms attached to code you did not write, where they carry an obligation.
provenance  cosign, notation                                         Check that a container image is signed by the identity this descriptor expects.
sast        semgrep, gosec*                                          Find patterns in a repo's own code that let somebody in (static analysis).
sca         trivy-fs, govulncheck†, grype-fs*, mend-sca*, retirejs*  Find known flaws in the libraries a repo depends on (software composition analysis).
secrets     gitleaks                                                 Find passwords and keys committed by accident, history included.
threats     urlhaus, virustotal*                                     Check whether a component's hosts appear on public lists of known-hostile addresses.
tls         draugr-tls                                               Check the certificates and encryption a component's running endpoints offer visitors.

PUBLISHERS  (who each scanner comes from)
  Origin               Scanners
  draugr               draugr-headers, draugr-k8s-policies, draugr-tls, sarif-import
  Go team              govulncheck
  RetireJS             retirejs
  VirusTotal (Google)  virustotal
  abuse.ch             urlhaus
  anchore              grype, grype-fs
  aquasecurity         kube-bench, kube-bench-job, trivy, trivy-config, trivy-fs, trivy-license
  gitleaks             gitleaks
  mend.io              mend-licenses, mend-sca
  notaryproject        notation
  projectdiscovery     nuclei
  prowler-cloud        prowler
  securego             gosec
  semgrep              semgrep
  sigstore             cosign
Draugr executes third-party tools rather than bundling them, so each stays under its own
license. `draugr tools list` shows which are installed and where.

* opt-in scanner. Enable with config.controls.<control>.<scanner>.enabled: true in the Saga.
† reachability analyzer. Enable with config.reachability.analyzers: [<scanner>] in the Saga.

EFFECTS  (scanners that do more than read)
  Scanner         Effect      What happens
  cosign          disclosure  the digest of each image checked, to the registry holding it and, where a signature carries no inclusion proof, to the Sigstore transparency log
  draugr-headers  network     sends an HTTP GET to the endpoint and follows its redirects
  draugr-tls      network     opens TLS connections to the endpoint, one handshake per protocol version tested
  kube-bench-job  mutate      creates a short-lived Job in the cluster and deletes it when the scan finishes
  kube-bench-job  privilege   that Job runs with hostPID and mounts host paths read-only, which is what reading a node's kubelet and control-plane configuration requires
  mend-licenses   disclosure  uploads this component's resolved dependency inventory to Mend, names, versions, checksums, and the absolute paths they were found at
  mend-licenses   mutate      creates or updates a project inside your Mend product, which outlives the scan
  mend-sca        disclosure  uploads this component's resolved dependency inventory to Mend, names, versions, checksums, and the absolute paths they were found at
  mend-sca        mutate      creates or updates a project inside your Mend product, which outlives the scan
  notation        disclosure  the digest of each image checked, to the registry holding it. No transparency log is involved: a Notary Project signature is verified against certificates on this machine
  nuclei          network     sends probe traffic to the endpoint, which is lawful only against systems you own or have written permission to test
  nuclei          mutate      with intrusive: true, runs the templates tagged intrusive, some of which change the endpoint, such as creating a user or uploading a file with PUT
  trivy-fs        disclosure  with resolveMaven: true, sends the group, artifact and version of each dependency a pom.xml declares, and of theirs, to Maven Central and the repositories the pom names
  urlhaus         disclosure  sends each host's name to abuse.ch to ask whether it is known to serve malware, a third party learns the hostname
  virustotal      disclosure  sends each host's domain to VirusTotal, a Google service, to read what their engines already say about it
Effects marked mutate or privilege do not run until accepted. List them under
config.allowEffects in your Saga, or pass --allow-effects.

What each scanner accepts in its Saga block:

  cosign
    no options, configured by choosing it; any other key is an error

  draugr-headers
    no options, configured by choosing it; any other key is an error

  draugr-k8s-policies
    no options, configured by choosing it; any other key is an error

  draugr-tls
    Option           Type     What it does
    expiryErrorDays  integer  Report an error when the certificate expires within this many days. Defaults to 14.
    expiryWarnDays   integer  Report a warning when the certificate expires within this many days. Defaults to 30. Lower it for endpoints with automated renewal so the gate only fires when renewal has failed.

  gitleaks
    Option   Type     What it does
    config   string   Path to a Gitleaks TOML rules file, relative to where Draugr runs, for a ruleset shared across repositories. It replaces each scanned repository's own .gitleaks.toml. Without it, that file applies, or Gitleaks' built-in rules where the repository has none.
    history  boolean  Also scan the repository's commit history, in addition to the working tree. Off by default: it needs a full clone rather than a shallow one, so it is slower on a large repository. A secret committed and later removed is still fetchable by anyone who can clone, so it is still compromised, this is what finds it. History findings are marked as such, because they name the path the secret had when it was introduced, and are kept only when that path would be checked out by this component's paths and ignore. A secret still in the tree at the same path is reported once, as the tree finding.

  gosec
    Option   Type   What it does
    exclude  array  Skip these gosec rules, by ID, e.g. ["G104"]. For rules that do not apply to the codebase, a finding you have judged and accepted belongs in exclusions, where it stays in the report marked suppressed.
    include  array  Run only these gosec rules, by ID, e.g. ["G101", "G204"]. Everything else is skipped.
    tags     array  Go build tags to compile with, e.g. ["integration"]. Code behind a tag gosec does not build is code it does not analyze.

  govulncheck
    no options, configured by choosing it; any other key is an error

  grype
    Option  Type     What it does
    byCve   boolean  Report a finding under its CVE rather than the advisory ID the source used, where a CVE exists. On by default. Turn it off to see the advisory identifier Grype matched on.

  grype-fs
    Option  Type     What it does
    byCve   boolean  Report a finding under its CVE rather than the advisory ID the source used, where a CVE exists. On by default. Turn it off to see the advisory identifier Grype matched on.

  kube-bench
    Option     Type    What it does
    configDir  string  Path to kube-bench's cfg/ tree of benchmark definitions. Needed when the binary is on PATH and its cfg lives beside it rather than in /etc/kube-bench/cfg.
    targets    string  Comma-separated kube-bench targets to run, e.g. "master,node". Defaults to policies, the section a scan from outside the control plane can answer.

  kube-bench-job
    Option        Type    What it does
    image         string  kube-bench image the Job runs. Pin it to a digest for a reproducible benchmark, and to an internal mirror where the cluster cannot reach a public registry.
    namespace     string  Namespace to create the Job in. It must already exist; Draugr does not create namespaces.
    nodeSelector  string  Node selector for the Job, as comma-separated key=value pairs. Use it to benchmark a specific node pool.
    targets       string  Comma-separated kube-bench targets to run, e.g. "master,node".
    timeout       string  How long to wait for the Job to finish, e.g. "5m".

  mend-licenses
    Option                   Type    What it does
    productToken (required)  string  The Mend product this component reports into. Required. It identifies a product and grants no access, so it belongs in the descriptor rather than the environment.
    deny                     array   SPDX identifiers that fail the gate, e.g. ["AGPL-3.0-only", "SSPL-1.0"]. "unknown" matches any license the scanner could not identify.
    project                  string  Prefix for each Mend project name, <project>-<repository>. Each repository reports into its own project, suffixed with the paths and an 8-character hash when the component sets paths or ignore. Unset, names start at the repository.
    resultTimeout            string  How long to wait for Mend to finish analyzing an upload, e.g. "15m". Raise it for large monorepos, where the agent's upload finishes long before the analysis does.
    settings                 object  Unified Agent configuration keys, passed through verbatim. The agent runs over a checkout already restricted to the component's paths and ignore.
    warn                     array   SPDX identifiers reported as warnings rather than failures, e.g. ["GPL-3.0-only"]. "unknown" matches any license the scanner could not identify.

  mend-sca
    Option                   Type    What it does
    productToken (required)  string  The Mend product this component reports into. Required. It identifies a product and grants no access, so it belongs in the descriptor rather than the environment.
    project                  string  Prefix for each Mend project name, <project>-<repository>. Each repository reports into its own project, suffixed with the paths and an 8-character hash when the component sets paths or ignore. Unset, names start at the repository.
    resultTimeout            string  How long to wait for Mend to finish analyzing an upload, e.g. "15m". Raise it for large monorepos, where the agent's upload finishes long before the analysis does.
    settings                 object  Unified Agent configuration keys, passed through verbatim. The agent runs over a checkout already restricted to the component's paths and ignore.

  notation
    no options, configured by choosing it; any other key is an error

  nuclei
    Option       Type     What it does
    concurrency  integer  Most templates run at once. 1 to 1000.
    excludeTags  array    Template tags to skip, e.g. ["wordpress"]. Lowercase. Added to headers and intrusive, which are skipped whatever this lists, intrusive until intrusive is true.
    interactsh   boolean  Run the templates that detect out-of-band callbacks. They have the endpoint contact Nuclei's public interactsh servers (oast.pro, oast.live, oast.site, oast.online, oast.fun, oast.me), and Nuclei polls them. false skips those templates and contacts none of them.
    intrusive    boolean  Also run the templates tagged intrusive, some of which write to the endpoint, such as creating a user or uploading a file with PUT. Declares the mutate effect, so config.allowEffects must list mutate.
    rateLimit    integer  Most requests sent per second, across the whole scan. 1 to 10000.
    retries      integer  Times a failed request is sent again. 0 to 10.
    tags         array    Run only the templates carrying one of these tags, e.g. ["cve", "exposure"]. Lowercase. headers is refused, and intrusive needs intrusive: true.
    templates    array    Template files or directories to run instead of the community set, relative to where Draugr runs. Tag exclusions apply to them too, so a template of yours that writes to the endpoint should carry the intrusive tag.
    timeout      string   How long to wait for each response, in whole seconds, e.g. "30s". 1s to 10m.

  prowler
    Option      Type    What it does
    compliance  string  The Prowler compliance framework to run, such as cis_4.0_gcp. Defaults to cis_5.0_gcp. `prowler gcp --list-compliance` lists them.

  retirejs
    no options, configured by choosing it; any other key is an error

  semgrep
    Option  Type    What it does
    config  string  Ruleset to run: a Semgrep registry ref (e.g. p/owasp-top-ten), a URL, or a rules file or directory on disk, resolved relative to where Draugr runs. Defaults to p/default.

  trivy
    Option        Type   What it does
    dbRepository  array  OCI repositories to pull the vulnerability database from, in priority order. Point it at an internal mirror where the runner cannot reach a public registry.
    pkgTypes      array  Which package types to analyze. Defaults to both. Narrow it to ["library"] when the OS layer is somebody else's responsibility, a base image maintained by a platform team, so the report covers what this component controls. One of: os, library.

  trivy-config
    Option      Type   What it does
    checks      array  Paths to Rego check files, or directories of them, relative to where Draugr runs. Adds your own misconfiguration rules to Trivy's built-in ones. Without namespaces, every check must declare a package, and the package's first name is the namespace it runs under.
    namespaces  array  Top-level Rego package names whose checks Trivy evaluates, e.g. ["user"] for package user.tags. Unset, Draugr derives the list from the package lines in checks; set, only the listed namespaces run.

  trivy-fs
    Option             Type     What it does
    dbRepository       array    OCI repositories to pull the vulnerability database from, in priority order. Point it at an internal mirror where the runner cannot reach a public registry.
    detectionPriority  string   How Trivy decides which version of a dependency is installed. One of: precise, comprehensive.
    filePatterns       array    More files for an analyzer to read, each analyzer:regex, e.g. ["pip:requirements-.*\\.txt"]. Trivy's pip analyzer reads only requirements.txt.
    includeDevDeps     boolean  Deprecated: write config.controls.sca.devDependencies instead. true is read as devDependencies: report. Trivy reads development dependencies on every run.
    pkgTypes           array    Which package types to analyze. Defaults to both. Narrow it to ["library"] when the OS layer is somebody else's responsibility, a base image maintained by a platform team, so the report covers what this component controls. One of: os, library.
    resolveMaven       boolean  Fetch each dependency's pom from Maven Central and the repositories the pom names, to read a pom.xml's transitive dependencies. Sends every dependency's coordinates to them. Ignored with --offline.

  trivy-license
    Option  Type     What it does
    deny    array    SPDX identifiers that fail the gate, e.g. ["AGPL-3.0-only", "SSPL-1.0"]. "unknown" matches any license the scanner could not identify.
    full    boolean  Also read LICENSE files and source headers, not only package metadata. Finds licenses no manifest declares, and is markedly slower, it reads every file rather than the dependency list.
    warn    array    SPDX identifiers reported as warnings rather than failures, e.g. ["GPL-3.0-only"]. "unknown" matches any license the scanner could not identify.

  urlhaus
    no options, configured by choosing it; any other key is an error

  virustotal
    Option             Type     What it does
    requestsPerMinute  integer  Override the assumed API allowance. Defaults to the public tier's 4. Raise it only to what your key actually permits, exceeding VirusTotal's limit risks losing access, and their stated penalty for terms violations is a permanent ban.

Enable a control under config.controls.<name> (or per component) in your Saga.
